<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TButton</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TButton" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TButton</tt> creates a click button on a Web page. The button's caption is specified by <tt>Text</tt> property. A button is used to submit data to a page. <tt>TButton</tt> raises two server-side events, <tt>OnClick</tt> and <tt>OnCommand</tt>, when it is clicked on the client-side. The difference between <tt>OnClick</tt> and <tt>OnCommand</tt> events is that the latter event is bubbled up to the button's ancestor controls. An <tt>OnCommand</tt> event handler can use <tt>CommandName</tt> and <tt>CommandParameter</tt> associated with the event to perform specific actions.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
Clicking on button can trigger form validation, if <tt>CausesValidation</tt> is true. And the validation may be restricted within a certain group of validator controls according to <tt>ValidationGroup</tt>.
</p>
<h2>Button type</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The <tt>ButtonType</tt> property writes the <tt>type</tt> attribute of the rendered
element. It defaults to <tt>Submit</tt>.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Value</th><th>Renders</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Submit</tt></td>
	<td><tt>type="submit"</tt></td>
	<td>The button submits the form. This is the default.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Reset</tt></td>
	<td><tt>type="reset"</tt></td>
	<td>The browser restores every field in the form to its initial value.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Button</tt></td>
	<td><tt>type="button"</tt></td>
	<td>The browser takes no action of its own.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
A button whose type is not <tt>Submit</tt> reaches its handler through a client script.
The control registers that script when it carries an <tt>OnClick</tt> or an
<tt>OnCommand</tt> handler and <tt>EnableClientScript</tt> is true. Setting
<tt>EnableClientScript</tt> to false leaves such a button without a way to post back.
</p>

<h2>Rendered element</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.3.0" />

<p class="block-content">
The <tt>ButtonTag</tt> property selects which HTML element the button renders. It takes
<tt>Input</tt> or <tt>Button</tt>, and it defaults to <tt>Input</tt>.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Value</th><th>Renders</th><th>Where the caption goes</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Input</tt></td>
	<td><tt>&lt;input&gt;</tt></td>
	<td><tt>Text</tt> becomes the <tt>value</tt> attribute. The element has no body content.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Button</tt></td>
	<td><tt>&lt;button&gt;</tt></td>
	<td><tt>Text</tt> becomes body content, and the control renders any other body content the template gives it.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
An <tt>&lt;input&gt;</tt> element carries plain text only. A <tt>&lt;button&gt;</tt>
element holds markup, so a caption that needs an image or styled text uses
<tt>ButtonTag="Button"</tt>.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TButton ButtonTag="Button" OnClick="save"&gt;
  &lt;com:TImage ImageUrl="save.png" AlternateText="" /&gt;
  Save
&lt;/com:TButton&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>The default button</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>IsDefaultButton</tt> makes the button answer the return key. A
<a href="?page=Controls.Panel">TPanel</a> sets it on the button its own
<tt>DefaultButton</tt> property names, so a template usually configures this from the
panel rather than on the button.
</p>

<h2>Custom attributes</h2>

<p class="block-content">
A template adds any HTML attribute to the rendered element through the
<tt>Attributes</tt> collection. The attribute name follows <tt>Attributes</tt> in the
tag.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TButton Text="Save" Attributes.formnovalidate="formnovalidate" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

